            {
              "id": "2bd92ad8-3d02-3fd9-802f-7653db8ad6ef",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak hash function like MD5. MD5 is considered weak because it is susceptible to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, thus compromising the integrity of the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not susceptible to known collision attacks. Additionally, it is important to keep up to date with the latest cryptographic research and standards, as the security of hash functions can change over time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a stronger hash function. Here is an example of how to use SHA-256 with the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3305ce50-fc3d-3b7a-8ed9-d34f5f7d4e6d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing, encryption, or digital signatures. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak cryptographic algorithm like MD5 for hashing. MD5 is considered insecure as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, making it easier for an attacker to reverse-engineer the original input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash algorithm such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are not susceptible to the same vulnerabilities as MD5.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a more secure hash algorithm. The specific library and function to use will depend on your specific use case and the libraries available to you. Here is an example of how you might use the OpenSSL library to compute a SHA-256 hash:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nstd::string computeSHA256(const std::string& input) {\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, input.c_str(), input.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e033b0bc-afd4-3a1d-a01b-69fc2d1c3c21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e17389e5-115a-3f48-923a-b2f3caaee779",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak hash function like MD5. MD5 is considered insecure for many cryptographic functions as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, making it easier for an attacker to reverse-engineer the original input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function. SHA-256 is a good choice for most applications. It's also important to keep up to date with the latest cryptographic standards and recommendations, as what is considered secure can change over time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5 hash function with a more secure one, such as SHA-256. Here's an example of how you might do this using the OpenSSL library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\n// usage\nkernel[\"sha256_sum\"] = sha256(input);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the SHA-256 hash function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "e17389e5-115a-3f48-923a-b2f3caaee779"
                ]
              }
